    "https://employment.ric.edu/postings/11204": {
      "title": "Teacher of the Visually Impaired",
      "description": "Rhode Island College Employment Opportunities | Teacher of the Visually Impaired Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Annual Security Report Applicant FAQs Teacher of the Visually Impaired Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Please be advised that Rhode Island College is unable to provide visa sponsorship for this position. Candidates must have authorization to work in the US without the need for employer sponsorship. Posting Details Position Information Posting Number NC00880 Position Title Teacher of the Visually Impaired Division Academic Affairs Division Department Name Sherlock Center, Vision Education and Services Program Position Type Staff - Non Classified Position Overview The Sherlock Center invites applications for a Teacher of the Visually Impaired, Paul V. Sherlock Center on Disabilities. The individual will work as an itinerant vision educator conducting vision assessments, providing vision instruction and other duties assigned by the director. The Paul V. Sherlock Center on Disabilities, founded at Rhode Island College in 1993, is a University Center for Excellence in Developmental Disabilities ( UCEDD ). UCEDDs are evidence driven and designed to advance policies, practices and research that improve the health, education, social and economic well-being of people with disabilities, their families, and their communities. The mission of the Sherlock Center is to promote membership of individuals with disabilities in school, work and the community. Primary Purpose This teacher will be responsible for direct specialized instruction as well as indirect services to students who are blind or visually impaired (ages 3-21) as a member of a trans-disciplinary team in a variety of settings. Essential Duties Assessment and Evaluation: obtain and interpret for the TEAM , all eye related information, conduct and interpret functional vision and learning media assessments, recommend and collaborate in additional assessments as needed (low vision, physical therapy, occupational therapy, psychological, speech and language, etc). Participate in the development and implementation of Individualized Education Plans as a member of the trans-disciplinary TEAM . Provide direct, specialized instruction of developmental and compensatory skills as they relate to vision and visual impairment. These may include: Braille reading and writing (pre-Braille, Grade 1 and 2, Nemeth, etc.), concept development, visual efficiency, print adaptations and learning devices, handwriting, typewriting, listening skills, study skills, tactual skills, communication development, motor development, activities of daily living, physical education, leisure and recreation, career education, and transition skills. Provide training and consultation to teachers, therapists, and support staff on the impact of vision loss as well as educational accommodations, modifications, and strategies necessary for full access to the general core curriculum and the expanded core curriculum. Prepare, obtain, and disseminate specialized materials and books – with an emphasis on developing and communicating timelines with appropriate personnel to ensure that materials are available when needed. Be knowledgeable of state regulations and procedures for referrals, assessments, IEP development, and progress reports. Consult with students, parents, teachers, counselors, and other school personnel regarding private and public agencies, schools, and resources within the community for students who are blind or visually impaired. Participate in developing, coordinating, and/or providing the continuum of related services needed by students who are blind or visually impaired. Collaborate with school technology personnel as well as agencies and vendors to assist in the assessment and use of assistive technology. Maintain records of student assessments, IEPs, reviews, release forms, etc. following policies regarding confidentiality. Maintain records of material and equipment requests, census information, etc. Participate in professional development activities. Occasional Duties Other related work as assigned by the RIVESP Program Coordinator and/or the Director of the Paul V. Sherlock Center on Disabilities. Required Skills, Knowledge, and Abilities Comprehensive and up-to-date knowledge of best practices for children who are blind or visually impaired. Must be proficient in Grade II Braille and Nemeth Code. Ability to conduct functional vision and learning media assessments of children who are blind or visually impaired. Ability to develop, support, and monitor appropriate learning environments for students who are blind or visually impaired. This may include in-service training of school personnel, assisting with the training and monitoring of instructional assistants, and/or directly assisting the student in academic subjects and activities. Knowledge of core general curriculum and expanded core curriculum. Possession of technical skills in the use of assistive technology, computers, specialized devices, and effective instructional techniques. Strong organizational, communication, and report writing skills as necessary in an itinerant model. Required Qualifications Education: Bachelor’s degree in Education of children who are blind or visually impaired. Certifications: Eligible for Rhode Island Teacher Certification as a Teacher of Children who are blind and visually impaired. Experience: Experience with children who are blind or visually impaired. Preferred Qualifications Master’s Degree Does this position have Direct Reports? No Environmental Conditions The employee is not exposed to any adverse environmental conditions. Position Details FLSA Exempt Salary Grade Not Applicable Full Time / Part Time Full-time Proposed Starting Salary Range $59,238 - $108,049 (Salary calculated based on years of experience) Work Schedule Full-time, calendar year,182 school days to include summer school schedule Benefits Full-time employee benefits include: Comprehensive Health Benefits and Rewards for Wellness Incentives Generous Paid Time Off Family/Parental Leave Tuition Waivers at RIC , CCRI & URI Retirement Plan Long-term Disability Coverage Variety of voluntary add-on’s For a complete listing, visit: http://www.employeebenefits.ri.gov/benefits/new/index.php Reports To (Title) Associate Director, Paul V. Sherlock Center on Disabilities Academic or Calendar Year Calendar Position Status Grant-funded End of Temporary or Limited Position Collective Bargaining Unit HUT Posting Information Number of Positions Available 1 Application Open Date 08/25/2026 Application Close Date Special Instructions Please be advised that Rhode Island College is unable to provide visa sponsorship for this position. Candidates must have authorization to work in the US without the need for employer sponsorship. EEO Statement Rhode Island College is an equal opportunity employer. The institution does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, disability religion, age, veteran status, or any other protected status under applicable law. Rhode Island College is committed to maintain a workplace and educational environment that supports equal opportunity, professionalism and respectful engagement for all community members. Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * Do you have a bachelor's degree in education of children who are blind or visually impaired? Yes No * Do you have experience working with children who are blind or visually impaired? Yes No * Are you eligible for the Rhode Island Teacher Certification as a Teacher of Children who are blind and visually impaired? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Resume Cover Letter Unofficial Transcripts Optional Documents Rhode Island College 600 Mount Pleasant Avenue Providence, RI 02908 (401) 456-8216 www.ric.edu RIC is an EEO/E-Verify Employer To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://employment.ric.edu/postings/11149": {
      "title": "Director, Project Management Office",
      "description": "Rhode Island College Employment Opportunities | Director, Project Management Office Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Annual Security Report Applicant FAQs Director, Project Management Office Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Please be advised that the college is unable to provide visa sponsorship for this position. Candidates must have authorization to work in the US without the need for employer sponsorship. Posting Details Position Information Posting Number NC00871 Position Title Director, Project Management Office Division Administration and Finance Division Department Name Administration and Finance Position Type Staff - Non Classified Position Overview Rhode Island College is seeking an experienced and strategic leader to establish and lead its Project Management Office ( PMO ) within the Division of Administration and Finance. This is an exciting opportunity to build a PMO from the ground up, shaping the college’s approach to project governance, portfolio management, and organizational transformation. The Director of the Project Management Office will drive the successful delivery of high-impact, cross-functional initiatives by implementing best-in-class project management practices, fostering collaboration across administrative and technology teams, and ensuring projects align with the College’s strategic priorities. Working closely with executive leadership, including the Assistant CIO and senior administrators, the Director will provide portfolio oversight, strengthen institutional project management capabilities, and promote a culture of accountability, transparency, and continuous improvement. Ideal candidates are collaborative leaders with a proven track record of building PMOs, managing complex project portfolios, and leading enterprise-wide initiatives in dynamic, matrixed organizations. This role offers the opportunity to make a lasting institutional impact by improving how projects are planned, prioritized, and delivered across Rhode Island College. Primary Purpose The Director of the Project Management Office ( PMO ) is responsible for establishing, leading and sustaining the PMO within Administration and Finance at the college. This role is responsible for developing governance frameworks, standardizing project management practices, and overseeing the institutional project portfolio to ensure initiatives are delivered on time, within scope, and aligned with strategic priorities. The Director builds and drives the adoption of consistent tools, processes, and delivery standards across a complex organization, while fostering a strong project management culture through coaching, training, and stakeholder engagement. This position serves as the central point of accountability for portfolio transparency, delivery performance, and the ongoing enhancement of project management capabilities across the college. Essential Duties Design, implement, and maintain a Project Management Office framework for the Division of Administration and Finance, establishing governance standards, delivery methodologies, and operating practices aligned with institutional needs and industry best practices. Define and standardize project governance processes across the division, including intake, prioritization, resource planning, risk management, status reporting, and post-implementation review. Build and manage the college’s project portfolio ensuring initiatives are prioritized, resourced, and delivering measurable value. Serve as hands-on project and program manager for complex, cross-functional initiatives, providing direct delivery leadership from initiation through closeout across any functional area within the division. Develop and maintain a portfolio dashboard and reporting cadence that provides division leadership with timely, accurate visibility into project status, risks, dependencies, and outcomes. Facilitate the development of project charters, scope documents, work plans, RACI matrices, and communication plans in partnership with project sponsors and functional area leaders. Partner with the Assistant CIO on IT transformation and enterprise technology initiatives, co-leading delivery on complex programs. Serve as the integration point between stakeholders, proactively identifying and resolving cross-functional dependencies, resource conflicts, and sequencing issues. Champion a project management culture across the college through coaching, mentoring, and targeted training for staff at all levels. Lead the evaluation, selection, and administration of project management tools and platforms, ensuring consistent adoption and use across the college portfolio. Develop and maintain resource capacity models to support data-driven decisions on project prioritization, staffing, and scheduling. Manage vendor and partner relationships on projects, monitoring deliverables, timelines, and contractual performance. Ensure all projects comply with institutional policies, applicable regulations, and relevant security and compliance standards. Provide the college’s Leadership Team with regular portfolio health reports and strategic progress updates, escalating issues requiring executive attention. Supervise and develop PMO staff as the PMO function grows. Occasional Duties Perform other duties as assigned. Required Skills, Knowledge, and Abilities Demonstrated experience building a Project Management Office in a complex, multi-function organization. Proven ability to manage a diverse portfolio of concurrent projects. Strong working knowledge of project management methodologies including waterfall, agile, and hybrid approaches, with the judgment to apply the right method to the right initiative. Experience serving as hands-on project and program manager for large, cross-functional enterprise or administrative initiatives. Excellent written and verbal communication skills, including the ability to prepare and present portfolio-level reports to VP and executive-level audiences. Strong analytical skills with demonstrated experience building and maintaining portfolio dashboards, metrics, and reporting frameworks. Proficiency with project management tools and platforms (e.g., Smartsheet, Jira, MS Project, or equivalent). Exceptional organizational skills, attention to detail, and ability to manage competing priorities across multiple functional domains under deadline pressure. Proven ability to build relationships and foster collaboration across diverse stakeholder groups without direct line authority. Sufficient familiarity with information technology systems and infrastructure to collaborate effectively with IT leadership and manage technology-related projects within the portfolio. High degree of professional integrity and commitment to confidentiality across sensitive administrative domains. Required Qualifications Education: Bachelor’s degree in Project Management, Information Systems, Business Administration, or a related field. Experience: Minimum of sevenyears of progressive project management experience; a minimum of three years managing a project portfolio or PMO function, with at least two years in a management or director-level role. Certification: Project Management Professional ( PMP ) certification Preferred Qualifications Master’s degree in Project Management, Business Administration, Public Administration, Information Systems, or a related field. Additional Project Management certifications (e.g., PMI - ACP , Lean Six Sigma, ITIL ) strongly preferred. Experience in higher education or a public sector environment, with an understanding of the governance, compliance, and stakeholder complexity these settings involve. Demonstrated experience managing projects across multiple administrative functions — such as finance systems, HRIS , facilities, or enterprise IT — within a single portfolio. Experience supporting the delivery of enterprise administrative system implementations ( ERP , SIS , HRIS , Finance platforms). Demonstrated experience coaching and mentoring staff at varying levels of project management maturity across non-technical and technical teams. Does this position have Direct Reports? Yes Environmental Conditions The employee is not exposed to known adverse environmental conditions Position Details FLSA Exempt Salary Grade 17NC Full Time / Part Time Full-time Proposed Starting Salary Range $145,000 - $155,000 Work Schedule Monday – Friday 8:30AM-4:30PM May require evening and weekend work as needed. Benefits Full-time employees enjoy an array of benefits including: Health and Wellness – Medical, Dental & Vision Wellness Incentives and Rewards Paid time Off – Vacation, Sick & Holiday Retirement Benefits Disability and Insurance Benefits Education Benefits – Tuition waivers for RIC , CCRI & URI For a complete listing, visit Employee Benefits . Reports To (Title) Assistant Vice President, Financial Operations Academic or Calendar Year Calendar Position Status Continuing End of Temporary or Limited Position Collective Bargaining Unit Non-union Posting Information Number of Positions Available 1 Application Open Date 08/03/2026 Application Close Date Special Instructions Please be advised that the college is unable to provide visa sponsorship for this position. Candidates must have authorization to work in the US without the need for employer sponsorship. EEO Statement Rhode Island College is an equal opportunity employer. The institution does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, disability religion, age, veteran status, or any other protected status under applicable law. Rhode Island College is committed to maintain a workplace and educational environment that supports equal opportunity, professionalism and respectful engagement for all community members. Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Do you have a Bachelor’s Degree in Project Management, Information Systems, Business Administration, or a related field? Yes No Do you have a Project Management Professional (PMP) certification? Yes No Do you have a minimum of seven years of progressive project management experience, a minimum of three years managing a project portfolio or PMO function, with at least two years in a management or director-level role? Yes No Applicant Documents Required Documents Resume Optional Documents Cover Letter Rhode Island College 600 Mount Pleasant Avenue Providence, RI 02908 (401) 456-8216 www.ric.edu RIC is an EEO/E-Verify Employer To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://employment.ric.edu/postings/11039": {
      "title": "Adjunct, Department of Educational Studies",
      "description": "Rhode Island College Employment Opportunities | Adjunct, Department of Educational Studies Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Annual Security Report Applicant FAQs Adjunct, Department of Educational Studies Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Please be advised that the college is unable to provide visa sponsorship for this position. Candidates must have authorization to work in the US without the need for employer sponsorship. Applications will be reviewed on an ongoing basis as instructional and supervisory needs arise. The Feinstein School of Education and Human Development welcomes applications from individuals committed to excellence in teaching, mentoring, supervision, and professional preparation. Posting Details Classification Information FLSA Exempt Rate Per Credit Hour $1602/credit hour Position Information Position Title Adjunct, Department of Educational Studies Department Name Feinstein School of Education and Human Development Academic or Calendar Year Academic School Name Feinstein School of Education and Human Development Position Overview The Feinstein School of Education and Human Development ( FSEHD ) at Rhode Island College prepares educators, counselors, school psychologists, wellness and exercise science, and youth development professionals who positively impact schools, communities, and the lives of children, youth, and adults. The FSEHD is committed to excellence, equity, inclusion, community engagement, and preparing highly qualified professionals throughout Rhode Island and beyond. The FSEHD invites applications from qualified professionals interested in serving as adjunct faculty teaching content, practicum, or seminar courses and/or supervising student teachers or interns across its undergraduate and graduate programs. Essential Duties Adjunct faculty may be assigned to teach undergraduate and graduate courses, supervise clinical experiences, or facilitate seminars. Department of Educational Studies – Programs: Secondary Education English, Mathematics, or History/Social Studies Foundations of Education: Introductory to Teaching ( FNED 101) and Learning Schooling for Social Justice ( FNED 246) World Language Education Assignments may include: Teaching undergraduate or graduate content courses. Teaching practicum, internship, or seminar courses. Serving as a college supervisor. Supervising student teachers or interns in PK-12 settings or counseling, youth development, or wellness and exercise community organizations. Contact : Charlie McLaughlin, Chair of the Department of Educational Studies Occasional Duties Perform other duties and responsibilities as assigned by the Dean of the FSEHD . Required Skills, Knowledge, and Abilities Required Qualifications Master’s degree. Relevant professional experience in the field. Appropriate licensure, certification, or credentials, where applicable. Experience working with diverse learners and communities. Strong communication, mentoring, and supervisory skills. Preferred Qualifications Doctorate Degree preferred. Environmental Conditions The employee is not exposed to known adverse environmental conditions. Posting Detail Information Posting Number A00068 Benefits Eligible? No Number of Positions Available Multiple Anticipated Start Date or Semester Posting Date 07/09/2026 Closing Date Open Until Filled No Special Instructions/Information for Applicants Please be advised that the college is unable to provide visa sponsorship for this position. Candidates must have authorization to work in the US without the need for employer sponsorship. Applications will be reviewed on an ongoing basis as instructional and supervisory needs arise. The Feinstein School of Education and Human Development welcomes applications from individuals committed to excellence in teaching, mentoring, supervision, and professional preparation. Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Applicant Documents Required Documents Cover Letter Unofficial Transcripts Curriculum Vitae Optional Documents Rhode Island College 600 Mount Pleasant Avenue Providence, RI 02908 (401) 456-8216 www.ric.edu RIC is an EEO/E-Verify Employer To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
